      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "A 70-year-old woman, who presented with pain and functional limitation of her right shoulder, without any previous traumatic incident, was evaluated in an emergency department. A shoulder x-ray was performed, and she received an intrajoint injection of an unspecified amount of triamcinolone. Forty-eight hours later, she noticed a papulovesicular and bullous eruption with a dermatomal distribution on her nape, supraclavicle, and left brachial and antebrachial regions (T1-T2-C5-C6-8). A diagnosis of herpes zoster (HZ) was made, for which she received valacyclovir 1 g every 8 hours for 10 days, pregabalin 75 mg every 8 hours, and topical acyclovir, with good response. The lesions and pain subsided, and results from general laboratory tests were normal or negative."},
